{"name":"Barbecue Sauce","hex":"#471c0f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"barbecue sauce","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fruity, tangy, usually brown  sauce, typically consisting of a vinegar or tomato base with spices and sweeteners, used as a basting sauce, marinade, or condiment, especially for barbecued meat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barbecue_sauce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barbecue sauce","blocks":[{"text":"Barbecue sauce is a sauce used as a marinade, basting, or topping for meat cooked in the barbecue cooking style, including pork, beef, and chicken. It is a ubiquitous condiment in the Southern United States and Australia which is used on many other foods as well."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Pork_steaks_cooking-1.jpg/330px-Pork_steaks_cooking-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbecue_sauce"},{"title":"Barbecue","blocks":[{"text":"Barbecue or barbeque is a term used with regional and national variations to describe various cooking methods, employing live fire and smoke to cook food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Barbecued_meats.jpg/330px-Barbecued_meats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbecue"},{"title":"Sauce","blocks":[{"text":"In cooking, a sauce is a liquid, cream, or semi-solid food, served on or used in preparing other foods. Most sauces are not normally consumed by themselves; they add flavour, texture, and visual appeal to a dish. Sauce is a French word probably from the post-classical Latin salsa, derived from the classical salsus 'salted'. Possibly the oldest recorded European sauce is garum, the fish sauce used by the Ancient Romans, while doubanjiang, the Chinese soy bean paste, is mentioned in Rites of Zhou 20."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lunch_sauces.jpg/330px-Lunch_sauces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauce"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Maulls24oz.jpg/500px-Maulls24oz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Maulls24oz.jpg/500px-Maulls24oz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bottles of Maull's barbecue sauce, a commercial St. Louis–style barbecue sauce","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaulls24oz.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Barbecue_Virginia_Barbacoa.jpg/500px-Barbecue_Virginia_Barbacoa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Barbecue_Virginia_Barbacoa.jpg/500px-Barbecue_Virginia_Barbacoa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The original Arawak term barabicu was used to refer to a wooden framework. Among the framework's uses was the suspension of meat over a flame.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarbecue_Virginia_Barbacoa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/British_Barbecue.jpg/500px-British_Barbecue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/British_Barbecue.jpg/1280px-British_Barbecue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/British_Barbecue.jpg/500px-British_Barbecue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A British barbecue including chicken kebabs, marinated chicken wings, sweetcorn, and an assortment of vege